  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 156 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nThursday night through Sunday night\nAssessment...medium (40-60%) confidence on rain occurring Friday\n\nThe first of two waves moves through the area Friday into Saturday. \nThis wave has respectable moisture associated with it and thus the \nrain chances during this time period look reasonably good. The model \nconsensus has 60-80% chances for rain which 'may' be on the high \nside.\n\nMoisture from the remnants of Vance move through the Midwest \nSaturday night into Sunday. Compared to yesterday, the bulk of the \nmoisture is now being shunted south of the area. This southward \nshift raises questions regarding the rain chances Saturday night \ninto Sunday. The model consensus has come down on the overall \nchances by 10 points and are now at 20-35%. If the southern shift on \nthis moisture plume holds, I would expect rain chances to further \ndecrease Saturday night into Sunday.\n\nMonday through Wednesday\nAssessment...medium (35-50%) confidence of a significant system\noccurring in the central CONUS\n\nIn the Monday through Wednesday time period, some models are \nadvertising a significant system while others are not. This \ndifference looks to be related to whether or not there is phasing \nbetween the northern and southern jets. IF phasing occurs, a more \nsignificant and slower system will be seen. IF no phasing occurs, \nthen a weaker and faster system would occur.\n\nThe model consensus has slowed down the system by 12 hours and is \nnow suggesting a Monday night into Tuesday night time frame for the \nbest rain chances. Given the two possible scenarios in play, the \nmodel consensus has a daily risk of rain from Monday through \nWednesday.",
